															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/beedee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(324750); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=324750&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609905574" alt="BeeDee" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=324750)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/beedee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(324750); return false;" >BeeDee</a></span> <span class="odate time_1242608695 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 May 2009 01:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-482045">
									

<p>Indeed. 39&nbsp;km isn't even out of Earth's atmosphere to any appreciable degree, though it's far enough up that you couldn't breathe up there.</p>
<p>I think calling the summit object a "satellite" is probably just too misleading and the changes that would be necessary to make it <em>into</em> a satellite are too great. For example, if it really was geostationary it'd be directly above the equator, so the rope would rise at an angle of somewhere between approximately 30 and 40 degrees (depending on where in Tibet the mountain is) from vertical. Furthermore, there'd be satellite collisions with it - this is one of the major risks faced by space elevator designs in real life.</p>
<p>I think it's best to just describe the summit object as an "object" of some sort. It's no more a satellite than a tethered balloon would be.</p>

							</div>

								
					
